{"id":"h240","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"A) Determine the pH of a solution with a hydrogen ion concentration of 3.5 × 10^-4. B) If a solution has a pH of 4.25, what is the hydrogen ion concentration?\nIs the correct answer \"A) 4.46, B) 6.6 × 10^-5 mole/liter\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The proposed answer is incorrect in both parts.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The pH for [H+] = 3.5 × 10^-4 is about 3.46, not 4.46.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A concentration of 3.5 × 10^-4 M is acidic, so a pH near 3.5 is expected.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"A pH of 4.46 would correspond to a concentration near 3.5 × 10^-5 M.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"For pH 4.25, [H+] is about 5.6 × 10^-5 mol/L, not 6.6 × 10^-5.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"6.6 × 10^-5 mol/L would give a pH of about 4.18, not 4.25.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The proposed numbers are of the right order of magnitude, which weakly favors the proposed answer.","confidence":0.2,"lean":1,"theta":0.3142,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The question requires specific values, and the calculations do not support the proposed ones, so the arithmetic decides the matter.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":-0.8,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":-0.7,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.4,"relation":"suppor